README-METAFONT -- rendering a font on this disk================================================The TeX tree ships thirty programs, and eight of them are about BITMAPfonts: virmf, inimf, gftype, gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vptovf and vftovp.Until the base was built there was nothing on this disk for any of themto read, and this file is what that took.The fonts that now ship-----------------------The sixteen fonts Plain TeX preloads are here as glyphs, rendered at 300dots per inch -- cmr5/7/10, cmbx5/7/10, cmmi5/7/10, cmsy5/7/10, cmsl10,cmti10, cmtt10 and cmex10.  Each is in SYS/TEX/FONTS/PK300 as a packed .pkand in SYS/TEX/FONTS as a .gf, because the drivers here read the two forms.With them in place every DVI driver on this disk RENDERS the sample story:dvialw, dvidjp, dviimp, dvijep and dvilj2 find the 300 dpi fonts outright,and dvieps, dvijet, dvimac, dvioki and dvitos -- which want otherresolutions -- scale the 300 dpi ones by nearest neighbor and render too.The "sets the text at zero size" that every driver used to report is gone.They were built the way the rest of this file describes, one virmf run andone gftopk each, in the CanonCX 300 dpi mode; SYS/TEX/FONTS/PK300/Makefileis the recipe for the whole 136-font set at any resolution.  A .pk is abitmap for one resolution, so for a printer that is not 300 dpi render thematching set, or let the driver scale as above.  That is still what TeX'sown MakeTeXPK does -- CMDS/TEXCMDS/maketexpk is here.The base, which now ships-------------------------Metafont needs a preloaded base the way TeX needs a format.  SYS/TEX/FORMATShas shipped plain.fmt, lplain.fmt and splain.fmt since the tree landed;SYS/TEX/MFBASES held only its Makefile and install.script, so virmf stoppedat    I can't find the default base file!`inimf' builds it out of SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS, in about a minute:    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; inimf < mf.in"where mf.in is the one line install.script asks for:    plain; \input modes; dumpThat writes plain.base, 80144 bytes.  It is now in SYS/TEX/MFBASES andvirmf finds it with nothing set.  Build your own the same way if you wanta base with local modes in it.install.script asks for a SECOND base, and that one also ships:    inimf '&plain cmbase dump'cmbase.base is 128312 bytes and is what the 117 Computer Modern sourcesunder MFINPUTS/CM are written against.  Without it cmr10 cannot be rendered,which is why every DVI driver on this disk warns that it cannot open a fontfile.  cmbase.mf is inside MFINPUTS/CM and the compiled-in MFINPUTS path doesnot descend into it, so building this one wants    export MFINPUTS=.:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTS/CM:/dd/SYS/TEX/MFINPUTSComputer Modern---------------    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'renders all 128 characters of cmr10 in seconds and writes cmr10.120gf,cmr10.tfm and cmr10.log.  gftopk packs the 7392-byte bitmap down to 2900.That is a real Computer Modern font, made here, out of sources that were onthis disk all along.THE JOB NAME COMES FROM THE COMMAND LINE.  The identical line fed on STANDARDINPUT renders exactly the same font and calls it `mfput' -- mfput.120gf,mfput.tfm -- whatever font it just made.  That is Metafont behaving asspecified and not a fault, but it looks like one, and it is also whyCMDS/TEXCMDS/maketexpk cannot finish: maketexpk's own recipe is right,    virmf '&plain \scrollmode; \mode:=%s; \mag:=%s; \input %s; \end'and it then reaches for `makdir', `del' and `attr' to file the result --three utilities of your own OS-9, which SYS/login loads from /h1 whenyour system is there.  Without them, run the virmf line yourself and use`gftopk'; that is all maketexpk was going to do.`epsonlo' is not a square-pixel mode, so pktype reports the horizontal andvertical resolutions differing and warns `aspect ratio not 1:1!'.  Pick amode out of MFINPUTS/modes.mf that matches whatever you are printing on.Rendering a font----------------logo10.mf is the smallest interesting font here -- the seven letters of theMETAFONT logo, and it needs no Computer Modern.  Pick a mode out ofMFINPUTS/modes.mf; `epsonlo' is a low-resolution one, which keeps thebitmap small.    echo "\\mode=epsonlo; mode_setup; input logo10" > /dd/tmp/run.mf    ksh -c "cd /dd/tmp; virmf < run.mf"Metafont answers with the character codes as it renders them and writesthree files: logo10.120gf (the bitmap), logo10.tfm (the metrics TeX reads)and logo10.log.`ksh -c "cd ..."' rather than plain `cd', because Metafont names its ownoutput files and writes them into the DATA directory -- and bash's `cd'does not move that.  See README-SHELLS.THE SEARCH PATHS DO NOT INCLUDE THE CURRENT DIRECTORY-----------------------------------------------------This is the one thing here that reads like a fault and is not.  The pathsare compiled into the binaries, and the font ones are    /dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS:/dd/USR/TEX/FONTSwith no leading `.', where the Metafont SOURCE path does begin with one.So gftype, gftopk, pktype, pktogf, vftovp and vptovf will tell you    logo10.120gf: GF file not foundabout a file that is in front of you.  Name the directory in the matchingenvironment variable and they find it:    export GFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export PKFONTS=/dd/tmp    export VFFONTS=/dd/tmp    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp:/dd/SYS/TEX/FONTS/TFMThe full set the binaries read is GFFONTS, PKFONTS, VFFONTS, TEXFONTS,TEXINPUTS, TEXFORMATS, TEXPOOL, MFINPUTS, MFBASES and MFPOOL.  An OUTPUTfilename is never searched for, so an absolute output path works with novariable set at all.Reading what Metafont wrote---------------------------    gftype -i logo10.120gfprints the glyphs as asterisks, which is the only way to SEE a character ona terminal.  `-m' adds the mnemonic opcodes beside them.    gftopk logo10.120gf logo10.120pkpacks it -- 608 bytes down to 364 for this font.  `pktype' prints the packedform back, glyphs included, and `pktogf' unpacks it.  The unpacked filecomes back 632 bytes rather than 608: pktogf rewrites the preamble commentas `GFtoPK 2.3 output from METAFONT output <date>' where Metafont wrote`METAFONT output <date>'.  The bitmap is unchanged; the header grew.    tftopl logo10.tfm logo10.plreads the metrics -- the file TeX itself would use, where the .gf is onlyfor the printer.Virtual fonts-------------A virtual font has no glyphs at all.  Each character is a short programsaying what to set from some REAL font, so one .vf can gather charactersfrom several fonts, shift them, or draw rules between them.    vptovf /dd/DOC/tex/sample.vpl /dd/tmp/s.vf /dd/tmp/s.tfm    vftovp s.vf s.tfm /dd/tmp/back.vplDOC/tex/sample.vpl is a one-character font written for this: it maps `A'onto cmr10's `A' and stops.  vftovp reads the binary pair back to the textit came from, and fills in cmr10's real checksum on the way, which thehand-written source left at zero.What this buys the DVI drivers------------------------------Eleven DVI drivers ship here.  With the 300 dpi fonts now in place every oneof them renders the sample story -- five at 300 dpi outright and five byscaling to their own resolution.  What follows is how that was done, and howto render at a resolution the shipped set does not cover.`dvijet' asks for 120 dpi for the sample DVI, and `epsonlo' is 120 dpi:    virmf '&cmbase' '\scrollmode; \mode:=epsonlo; \input cmr10; \end'    gftopk cmr10.120gf /dd/tmp/cmr10.120pk    export TEXFONTS=/dd/tmp    export FONTLIST=pk    dvijet story.dviThe page goes from 3138 bytes to 7350 -- the difference is real glyphrasters.  dvijet says `---using nearest neighbor [... [120 dpi]] instead'even when the match is exact; that is how it reports finding a font by thesecond of its two filename forms, not a complaint.Render each font the document actually uses.  The sample wants cmsl10 andcmbx10 as well as cmr10, and the ones you do not render stay zero-size.SYS/TEX/FONTS/PK300 now holds the sixteen Plain TeX fonts at 300 dpi andSYS/TEX/FONTS holds their .gf, so the sample renders with nothing left tobuild; PK144 is still empty, for whoever wants 144 dpi.`dvips' USED to be the one this could not rescue, and its trouble wasnever fonts: it stopped at `Couldn't find header file tex.pro' because no.pro file was anywhere on this disk.  Its seven PostScript prologues shipnow, in SYS/TEX/DVIPS, out of the same archive as its source -- color,crop, finclude, special, tex, texc and texps.  Measured 2026-09-12:`dvips mg_doc.dvi -o mg.ps' writes 165,508 bytes of PostScript over 38pages, with `<tex.pro>' named in its own transcript.Fonts are the only thing left, and they behave as fonts do: a size thathas not been built is reported (`Font cmbx10.432pk not found, characterswill be left blank') and dvips offers to make it.  `-M' tells it not to.